8483074,2024-03-05,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Information belongs to someone else,"To Whom It May Concern : I noticed in my consumer report there is a charge off reporting to my credit report by the three major credit reporting agencies. The charge off is reporting and its illegal according to the IRS cancelled debt rules. IRS cancelled debt is income. I attached a screenshot of the IRS cancelled debt general rules.

Section 1681 of the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) actually pertains to the responsibilities of consumer reporting agencies regarding the accuracy of consumer reports. Under this law, it is illegal for consumer reporting agencies to include charge-offs, on a consumer 's credit report. Charge-offs are considered cancelled debt and according to the IRS cancelled debt is considered income, income can not be reported in a consumer report. The document attached is a screenshot of the IRS cancelled debt general rules. Any attempt to report charge-offs on a consumer report in violation of the FCRA 's guidelines outlined in section USC 1681 is considered illegal. Compliance with the FCRA is crucial to protecting consumers ' rights and ensuring the accuracy of their credit information.

