8514391,2024-03-10,Credit card,General-purpose credit card or charge card,Fees or interest,Unexpected increase in interest rate,"In XXXX I opened a new checking account at Wells Fargo and received a XXXX  card and XXXX card. The XXXXt card was described as interest-free. I do not recall whether I was specifically told that the interest-free period was for a limited time. 
I used the XXXX card to acquire inventory for my new business. Later in XXXX, When my debt level reached around {$4000.00} or {$5000.00} XXXX visited my local Wells Fargo branch to verify that my debt totals were accurate and to determine how much interest I was paying, at which time I was reminded that my XXXX card was interest-free. Again, I have no memory of being told or reminded that the interest-free period was for a limited time on that occasion either, but I can not swear ( or prove ) that it didn't happen. 
In the summer of XXXX I began to receive from Wells Fargo offers to increase my credit limit. Wells Fargo must have contacted me XXXX XXXX XXXX times encouraging me to take action to raise my credit limit, which I ignored, as I did not want my debt to spiral out of control. Then I was sent notice that my credit limit had been raised unilaterally by Wells Fargo. All of these notices came in the form of either text messages or specific emails from Wells Fargo. 
In XXXX of XXXX the interest-free period expired and I was charged about {$350.00} interest on a balance of around {$18000.00}. I did not receive any text message or email from XXXX XXXX bringing my attention to the fact that the introductory interest-free period had expired, or that I was incurring what I would call major interest fees. ( {$350.00} might be a small matter to some, but I currently work in shipping at {$15.00} an hour, so to me that {$350.00} represents about 3 days of labor. ) I did not check my account statements in XXXX and missed this charge. Around this time, I began to receive unsolicited text message offers from unkown debt consolidation providers, which I thought were just junk mail. 
On XX/XX/XXXX, I first became aware that I was paying interest on this XXXX card, {$350.00} in XXXX and {$350.00} in XXXX, and that about {$700.00} in interest charges had been withdrawn from my account. I immediately called the number on the back of my credit card, explained the situation, was told that my case would be escalated and that I would receive a call back within XXXX  business days. On XXXX  XX/XX/XXXX in the late afternoon, having received no communication from Visa, I visited my local branch to attempt to stop the continuing accumulation of interest charges and to discuss ways to address the situation. One of the bank employees was very helpful in assisting me in communicating with Visa. This is when I discovered a few unpleasant facts : 1 ) I was told that while I could put a stop on one-time purchases, that stop would not apply to any subscriptions or other recurring payments I had put on the card. ( To me this is nightmarish, and still find it hard to believe that under the laws of our country, even if I as a consumer say enough or stop or no mas, I can't put the full brakes on the relationship, like a runaway train or a monster that can not be killed. ) So to stop falling further behind in interest payments, I felt I had no choice but to lean on a family member to pay off the account completely, which we did. In the process of doing so, we discovered 2 ) that Visa was charging another {$300.00} for XXXX interest, for a total of about {$1000.00} in interest charges. 
