My argument is based on Visa and Wells Fargo 's PATTERN OF COMMUNICATION. When they wanted to increase my credit limit, they had no trouble getting my attention through text messages and emails that ( like most people ) I check daily. And when the hammer fell and interest began accruing, somehow the debt consolidators out there knew more about my needs than I did, and their messages and offers were right in my face. How did they know I had a debt problem? How did they acquire that information I wonder? Does Visa make my personal financial information readily available? On the other hand, when it comes to Terms and Conditions describing the expiration dates of promotional rates, or notifications that interest charges have begun, there are NO text messages or emails ; rather, these are much less visible in lengthy documents in monthly statements, like the fine print in the contract in the recent movie XXXX. Except this isn't a movie, this is our reality. 
