Once the Louisiana Department of Revenue explained what I had to do to rectify this situation, within two ( 2 ) hours, I made the required payment, and on Monday, XX/XX/XXXX, at approximately XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX, the Louisiana Department of Revenue sent a fax to Capital One Bank with the subject title of Levy Cancellation- My Full Name- Last Four Digits of my SS # ''. Based on the information I received from the Capital One Representative, I was under the impression that by Wednesday, I would have access to my bank accounts. 

The following day, I called Capital One Bank again ( Tuesday, XX/XX/XXXX, XXXXXXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX ) to check on the status of my levy cancellation. I was told by a Capital One Representative that there was no indication that the fax was received and to check again the following day. On Wednesday, XX/XX/XXXX, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX  ), I called the Louisiana Department of Revenue to inquire about the fax that was sent. The Revenue Dept. told me to call Capital One to verify that the fax sent on XX/XX/XXXX was sent to the correct number. I called Capital One Bank immediately to verify the number and then called the Revenue Department to find out that the levy cancellation fax sent to Capital One Bank on XX/XX/XXXX was sent to the correct fax number ( XXXX ). Again, I was told by Capital One Representatives that the fax was not yet received and to wait an additional day. Meanwhile, I continued to express that I was in a foreign country and needed access to my accounts. 

